  "title": "Ignore directives inside fenced code blocks",
  "file": "src/db/store.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Someone starts a CLI or UI run whose request pastes a Markdown code block containing an example line \"Closes #7\". The run links issue #7 and claims it, so the factory labels that unrelated issue in-progress and posts lifecycle comments on it.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/adj/p.ts  (imports closedIssues from src/db/store.ts and calls closedIssues(\"Do X.\\n\\n```text\\nCloses #7\\n```\\n\\nCloses #894\"))",
    "output": "fenced: [ 7, 894 ]"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, src/db/store.ts:341-346 is `[...prompt.matchAll(/(?:^|\\n)[ \\t]*closes[ \\t]+#([1-9][0-9]*)[ \\t]*(?=\\r?\\n|$)/gi)]`. It checks each line on its own and has no idea of fenced blocks. The docs/GUIDE.md text added at head says \"Inline mentions, quotes, blockquotes and trailing prose do not link issues\", and the issue names \"the original request can quote code or issue text\" as a source of false links. A fenced block is the usual way to quote code, yet a directive inside one still links. The literal requirement (a whole line of its own) is met, so the input is uncommon and the severity is minor. Base matched the directive anywhere in the text, so the hole is not new, but the change left it open."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A user includes a fenced example containing a closing directive in their request. The factory claims the example\u2019s issue and posts labels and lifecycle comments there, potentially blocking intended work on that unrelated issue.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun /var/folders/dx/mlv6nn_15619ymmlszfmkywm0000gn/T/adjudicate-894.L69PjN/probe.ts",
    "output": "{\"name\":\"fenced\",\"head\":[8],\"base\":[8]}\n{\"name\":\"afterFence\",\"head\":[8,7],\"base\":[8,7]}\n{\"name\":\"unclosed\",\"head\":[8],\"base\":[8]}\n{\"name\":\"fenced\",\"linked\":[{\"repo\":\"owner/repo\",\"number\":8}],\"claimed\":true,\"labelPost\":true,\"startedPost\":true,\"calls\":3,\"logs\":[]}"
   },
   "evidence": "The probe imported HEAD\u2019s actual parser, Store and lifecycle reconciler, using an in-memory database and an injected GitHub runner without network access. Its fenced input was \"Example:\\n```text\\nCloses #8\\n```\". HEAD matches every qualifying physical line with /(?:^|\\n)[ \\t]*closes[ \\t]+#([1-9][0-9]*)[ \\t]*(?=\\r?\\n|$)/gi, without tracking fences. linkedIssues then maps those matches into issue links used by claims and lifecycle delivery. Base\u2019s /\\bcloses\\s+#([1-9][0-9]*)(?![\\w#])/gi also matches this example, so this is an unmet requirement concerning quoted code rather than a newly introduced behavior. The request identifies quoted code as a false-link source, and HEAD\u2019s GUIDE promises that quotes do not link. The later commit adds fence tracking, skips enclosed lines through a matching closing fence or end of input, and adds parser and lifecycle coverage."
  }
 },
