   "failure_scenario": "A user pastes a closing directive surrounded by nonbreaking spaces into a request. The factory silently omits its issue link and claim, allowing another run to start work on the same intended issue.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun /var/folders/dx/mlv6nn_15619ymmlszfmkywm0000gn/T/adjudicate-894.L69PjN/probe.ts",
    "output": "{\"name\":\"nbsp\",\"head\":[],\"base\":[8]}\n{\"name\":\"emspace\",\"head\":[],\"base\":[8]}\n{\"name\":\"separator\",\"head\":[],\"base\":[8]}\n{\"name\":\"ascii\",\"head\":[8],\"base\":[8]}\n{\"name\":\"nbsp\",\"linked\":[],\"claimed\":false,\"labelPost\":false,\"startedPost\":false,\"calls\":0,\"logs\":[],\"secondRunAccepted\":true,\"runCount\":2}\n{\"name\":\"emspace\",\"linked\":[],\"claimed\":false,\"labelPost\":false,\"startedPost\":false,\"calls\":0,\"logs\":[],\"secondRunAccepted\":true,\"runCount\":2}"
   },
   "evidence": "The NBSP input was \"\\u00a0Closes #8\\u00a0\"; the em-space input was \"\\u2003Closes #8\\u2003\". HEAD\u2019s actual parser returned no issues, and the real Store accepted both the padded request and a subsequent ASCII \"Closes #8\" request. HEAD restricts surrounding whitespace and the separator to [ \\t], although the request allows optional surrounding whitespace without that restriction. Base\u2019s unanchored expression accepts these surrounding characters, and its \\s+ separator also accepts Unicode whitespace. The later commit replaces these ASCII classes with [^\\S\\r\\n] in the shared parser and adds Unicode whitespace tests."
